   239	### 3.2 Stages
   240	
   241	The stage names follow the spec's. The spec registers no order between stages, only that the verdict does not
   242	depend on evaluation order. [SPEC:1237-1248; SPEC R-5.16:1250] There are ten stages after dispatch. The
   243	table presents ENC/SPEC material; it is not a newly fixed execution order (section 0.1, item 6).
   244	
   245	| Stage | What V1 does | Source |
   246	|---|---|---|
   247	| (a) Parse | Decode every frame, the manifest and `A_sig` under ENC §1 to §5. Reason codes are reported in ENC's order. | [ENC §1.4:172-190; ENC §3.1:241-248; ENC §5:385-394] |
   248	| (b) Type | Tag must be in T and be the expected one: `TAG_UNKNOWN`, `TAG_MISMATCH`. No wrapper stage: one layer only. | [ENC §2:215-218; A9:155] |
   249	| (c) Algorithm | A well-formed unknown `common.alg` gives `UNVERIFIABLE`. A malformed, missing or substituted id gives `INVALID`. | [ENC §3.2:276; SPEC V13-V15:1282-1284] |
   250	| (d) Key binding | Each presented key hashes to its slot's manifest fingerprint (`KEY_MISMATCH`). `manifest_sha256` equals the hash of the manifest frame. | [ENC §5.3:509; ENC §3.2:280] |
   251	| (e) Authority | DNS channel and git channel (section 6), the issuance window, possession proofs. | [A9:137; A9:338-340] |
   252	| (f) Signature set | Both slots are required, from the signed manifest, never from what the bundle presents. Common bytes are byte-equal across slots. | [SPEC V28-V31:1327-1330; ENC §3.4:303-320] |
   253	| (g) Integrity | `SHA256(A_sig)` is the digest the OTS evidence attests. Each committed `S` verifies over its committed `F`. Any difference from the committed tuple gives `RECEIPT_ANCHOR_MISMATCH`; a bad signature gives `SIG_INVALID`. | [ENC §5.3:498-511] |
   254	| (h) Temporal | The three-conjunct predicate at k = 6, plus the T rule (section 7). | [A9:138; SPEC V33-V36:1342-1346; SPEC R-5.19:1352] |
   255	| (i) Revocation | Not evaluated. The report says so. | [A9:248-252] |
   256	| (j) Partition | Section 3.3. | [SPEC R-5.25:1368] |
   257	
   258	- The issuance-window check is part of stage (e) (C4). [A9:338-340; ENC §7.1:636-643]
   259	- **Reported reason.** The reported reason is the first failed check in the **total check order** of section 15.7;
   260	  if none failed, the first unperformable check in that order. The order is a reporting order, not an execution
   261	  order. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G31] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` which reason code is reported when several different
   262	  checks fail", 2026-10-13.)*
   263	- **`DNS_*` record class.** A TXT RRset that is present is run through ENC §7's fifteen-step order; every failure is
   264	  `INVALID` with ENC's code, whether or not DNSSEC was performable. An absent TXT group is `UNVERIFIABLE /
   265	  DNS_EVIDENCE_MISSING`. ENC's `DNS_RRSET` zero case stays a codec vector, unreachable from a V1 container.
   266	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, G24] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` the verdict class of the `DNS_*` record rejections", 2026-10-13.)*
   267	- **Size gates.** An empty snapshot body or a payload over its cap gives `LENGTH_CAP`, before the JSON categories.
   268	  The gates are exact and separate: container 2^27 bytes, payload per ENC §1, snapshot body 2^24. [OWNER 2026-10-14,
   269	  G30] For an empty snapshot body this profile governs over ENC §6's `1 ≤ len` wording and over
   270	  `formal/p8/vectors/gen.py` (`decode_snapshot`, which reports `SCHEMA`): the outcome is `INVALID / LENGTH_CAP`.
   271	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, codified from diff-1, EA-20]
