   333	### 3.5 Verifier CLI and release-provisioning interface (instrument level)
   334	
   335	**Interface convention, not a verdict rule.** This subsection records how the conformance suite and the
   336	differential harness drive a verifier under test. It changes no verdict, reason or T, binds no production release,
   337	and gives way to the release artefact and release-signature format when those are decided (section 14.2 items 1,
   338	2). Both implementations of differential run 1 used it. [OWNER 2026-10-14, interface convention from diff-1,
   339	EA-01/EA-02/EA-03/EA-12] *The release format is now section 15.9. The TEST provisioning below is an instrument
   340	interface only: it is never a release, and an installed production verifier refuses it (section 15.9 R4).*
   341	[OWNER 2026-10-19, §14.2 item 2, `docs/V1-FREEZE-OPEN.md` §3]
   342	- **Invocation and compiled-in inputs (EA-01).** `CMD --trust <config> --bundle <bundle>`. The compiled-in trust
   343	  configuration hash and catalog (section 1, section 5) are provisioned for TEST by environment: the TEST release
   344	  record from `TESSERA_V1_TEST_RELEASE`, authenticated by the public key in `TESSERA_V1_TEST_RELEASE_KEY`, and the
   345	  catalog from `TESSERA_V1_TEST_CATALOG`. A config whose hash is not the one compiled by an authentically signed
   346	  release is refused (`TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`, no verdict).
   347	- **Request (EA-02).** The request (D4) is `strict` by default; `--request <value>` supplies any other value.
   348	- **Output and refusal (EA-03).** A verdict is one JSON object on stdout carrying the six stable fields, either at
   349	  top level or under `stable`, `result` or `report`; diagnostics may accompany it. *Instrument convention
   350	  [OWNER 2026-10-19, A10.1.1]:* the independent verifier prints `{"stable":<stable-output object>,
   351	  "utc_assessment":<section 7.4 object>}`; the conformance runner compares `utc_assessment` only where a case's
   352	  `expected.json` carries one. A startup refusal is a nonzero
   353	  exit with the refusal code on stdout or stderr and no six-field object.
   354	- **Release checker (EA-12).** `CMD --previous <config> --next <config>` prints `ACCEPT` or `REJECT` for the release
   355	  invariants of section 5.
   356	
   357	## 4. Issue order (issuer side, binding on the build)
   358	
   359	1. Fix the manifest and common metadata, including `declared_issue_time`.
   360	2. Build both frames.
   361	3. Sign each frame.
   362	4. Verify both signatures with the exact bytes to be committed.
   363	5. Freeze the signature bytes. Any re-framing happens before this point.
   364	6. Build `A_sig` and stamp its SHA-256. One stamp per receipt.
   365	7. Obtain and validate the confirmation and time evidence.
   366	8. Ship the complete receipt.
   367	
   368	[ENC §5.4:515-525; A9:278-281] That is eight steps in both sources. A retry that changes any committed byte needs a
   369	fresh stamp. The frame-only anchor is withdrawn, with no fallback. [A9:277; ENC §5.1:407-414]
   370	
   371	Issuer duties that are not verifier rules: an attempt whose T would exceed not-after is not shipped [A9:341-342]; the
   372	`gpg` signer refuses a frame whose `declared_issue_time` is more than ε from its clock [A9:327-330]; the issuer never
   373	re-authorizes a key [A9:260]; public publication of the commit stays an issuer duty [A9:413-418]; the issuer
   374	re-captures the DNSSEC snapshot before shipping if it fails the snapshot rule [DD:94].
   375	
   376	Further issuer duties [OWNER 2026-10-14]:
   377	- **Chain-order pre-commitment.** Do not issue in a window until the key-assertion commit's stamp has k = 6
   378	  confirmations and the receipt will anchor at a higher height (A9 C4). Before honest issuance the issuer obtains and
   379	  validates the git assertion's k = 6 proof. A receipt stamped after the issuer has observed h_git + 5 anchors above
   380	  it, so it passes the verifier's `h_git + 5 < h_receipt` rule (section 6.3). [G02; RB §5]
   381	- **Snapshot c3.** Check all three conjuncts on the snapshot's own proof before shipping; recapture or retry if they
   382	  fail. A snapshot stamped before the receipt need not confirm before it. [G06]
   383	- **DNSSEC support.** Validate the snapshot under this profile's algorithm set (section 6.1); do not ship a receipt
   384	  whose chain would be `DNSSEC_ALGORITHM_UNSUPPORTED`. [G16]
   385	- **Snapshot body.** Convert to the section 15.3 body before stamping. [G19]
   386	- **Signature output.** Both OpenPGP adapters emit signatures that satisfy the rule-O policy of section 8 before
   387	  signing is final and before stamping. [G11]
   388	- **Canonical container.** Emit the container as canonical JCS (section 15.1). [G17]
   389	- **Pre-ship checks for the 2026-10-14 r2 completions** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F07, r2-F08, r2-F09, r2-F10]: the
   390	  snapshot body carries no RRSIG with fewer Labels than its owner (section 15.3); the selected path of each of the
   391	  three proofs (receipt, snapshot, git) satisfies the transaction-shape subset and the conventional merkle suffix
   392	  (section 15.2), else the proof is re-stamped or the attempt abandoned; both OpenPGP adapters and the repository
   393	  signer emit type-16/type-33 subpackets only in the shapes section 8 allows. All 352 Bitcoin paths in the
   394	  repository's 129 proofs satisfy the shape and suffix rules (owner check, 2026-10-14); this is evidence, not a rule.
   395	
