   446	- **Root KSKs, three periods each.** The trust configuration records each KSK's signing and reliance periods
   447	  separately from its publication and revocation dates. [A9:153; A9:302-303] The instrument spike found that
   448	  conflating signing with publication falsely rejected every snapshot from 2026-10-11. [SR:73-78]
   449	- **Root rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time if some trusted KSK whose **reliance
   450	  period** contains that time validates it. Each KSK entry records signing-start, signing-end and a sourced reliance lifetime L; the reliance
   451	  interval is [signing-start, signing-end + L]. L is an acceptance-policy value from cited RRSIG validity evidence,
   452	  labelled observed, not a proven global maximum. The extension admits authentic pre-retirement evidence anchored
   453	  later and does not prove signing before retirement: a thief of a retired KSK can manufacture eligible evidence
   454	  during it (A9.5; AUTHOR ADOPTED 2026-10-13, kind CORRECT). *Dated correction 2026-10-13: the earlier wording
   455	  ("maximum validity … it made"; "usable for that long and no longer") overclaimed provenance and is withdrawn.* The rule is existential and
   456	  order-independent: a candidate that fails is informational, and the result is computed once, after every eligible
   457	  key has been tried. A trusted KSK that is published but did not sign is informational, never a failure.
