   855	## 7. Time
   856	
   857	### 7.1 The T rule
   858	
   859	- T is the time of the verified attestation at the **lowest block height** in the presented OTS proof; both T and
   860	  its confirmations come from that attestation. It is presentation-sensitive: a different presentation of the same
   861	  receipt can select a different T. [A9 C5 as revised 2026-10-14; DD:56, 98-99; OWNER 2026-10-14, G03] *Dated
   862	  correction 2026-10-14: this bullet previously read "T is the time of the earliest verified attestation", A9's
   863	  earlier wording. "Earliest" means chain order, not least nTime: header times are not monotone, and the two
   864	  selection rules can give different verdicts on the same proof.*
   865	- No lower bound and no priority. Header times are chain-time proxies. [A9:138]
   866	- The three conjuncts at k = 6 (`DepthK = k − 1 = 5`): `declared − ε ≤ anchor_time`; `anchor_time ≤ declared + δ`;
   867	  `confirmed_at ≤ declared + δ`. All three are evaluated together. [SPEC R-4.41:985; SPEC R-5.19:1352; SPEC
   868	  V33-V36:1342-1346; A9:99]
   869	- A real proof carries several attestations hours apart and nothing in the proof names T (M3). [FT:20; DD:98]
   870	- **T ≡ `anchor_time`.** For the receipt proof, T = `anchor_time` = nTime(h*), where h* is the lowest qualifying
   871	  height. The same value feeds conjuncts 1 and 2, the C4 window rule and the reported T. Two qualifying attestations
   872	  at the same height give the same T. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G03] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` one sentence stating whether
   873	  T is the `anchor_time` of the three conjuncts", 2026-10-13.)*
   874	- **`confirmed_at`** = nTime(h* + 5): the including block plus five successors, k = 6 (SPEC R-3.45, R-4.42). A
   875	  failure gives `INVALID`: `TIME_BEFORE_DECLARED_EPSILON`, `TIME_AFTER_DECLARED_DELTA` or `CONFIRMATION_LATE`.
   876	  Nonmonotone header times can give `confirmed_at` < T; that is not a failure, and there is no monotonicity check.
   877	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, G04]
   878	- **Qualifying ("verified") attestation.** A Bitcoin attestation at height h qualifies if and only if (a) the
   879	  bundled header at h exists and its merkle root equals the attestation message; (b) headers h … h+5 are all
   880	  bundled; (c) each equals its pin, `hex(reverse(SHA256d(header))) = pin[height]`; (d) for i = h+1 … h+5,
   881	  `header_i.prev = SHA256d(header_{i−1})`. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G05] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` what 'verified' means
   882	  for an attestation", 2026-10-13.)*
   883	- **Selection.** Attestation selection is existential over qualifying branches. A syntactically valid Bitcoin
   884	  attestation without a complete h..h+5 segment is nonqualifying, not a required failed check. It supplies no time or
   885	  trust fact. All supplied headers, including unused ones, are independently checked against pins (section 7.2).
   886	  The selected anchor, rather than every unverified attestation, is the referent of "anchors beyond the newest pin"
   887	  (A9 C6 as revised 2026-10-14). [OWNER 2026-10-14, G05]
   888	- **Proof outcomes.** A pinned bundled header at h whose merkle root ≠ the attestation message: `INVALID /
   889	  OTS_MERKLE_MISMATCH` (a contradicted branch fails the proof). No qualifying attestation: `UNVERIFIABLE /
   890	  HEADERS_MISSING` if any Bitcoin attestation is present, `UNVERIFIABLE / ANCHOR_PENDING_ONLY` if only pending ones;
   891	  the snapshot uses `SNAPSHOT_NOT_ANCHORED` and git `GIT_NOT_ANCHORED`. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G05]
   892	- **Time arithmetic.** Times are integer seconds since 1970-01-01T00:00:00Z without leap seconds; nTime is the
   893	  header's uint32; RFC 3339 fields convert by days-from-civil (proleptic Gregorian); comparisons are inclusive.
   894	  [OWNER 2026-10-14, G03]
   895	- **OTS and txid encoding**: section 15.2. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G20]
   896	- **txid scope and selected-height aggregation** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F08]: "§15.2's transaction-shape and
   897	  txid-equality check applies only to receipt_anchor. The snapshot/git proofs use the closed OTS grammar and header
   898	  qualification, with no txid comparison. Select h* before the receipt txid check; any satisfying qualifying path at
   899	  exactly h* passes that check. Never switch to a higher height merely to find a matching txid." **Owner choice
   900	  (fail-closed), stated separately:** the **transaction-shape and merkle-suffix subset** (section 15.2, parts 1, 2
   901	  and 4, without part 3's txid comparison) also applies to the selected height of the snapshot and git proofs; a
   902	  failure there is `INVALID / OTS_TX_SHAPE`, and any qualifying path at exactly that proof's h* satisfying the
   903	  subset passes. No receipt txid is compared with the snapshot or git proof; the three proofs normally anchor in
   904	  different transactions. *(Dated correction 2026-10-14: section 15.7's "OTS (as 11 …)" imported the receipt's
   905	  txid check into proofs that carry no txid.)*
   906	
   907	### 7.2 Header pins
   908	
   909	- Every bundle header must be in the pinned set. Otherwise the verdict is `UNVERIFIABLE / HEADERS_NOT_COVERED`.
   910	  There is no retarget logic, because the pins cover every accepted header. [A9:150; DD:99]
   911	- **Conflict.** A supplied header at a pinned height with a different hash gives `INVALID / HEADER_CONFLICTS_PIN`:
   912	  it is contradictory evidence. A9.1's "a bundle header outside the pins" is read as a header **at a height the pins
   913	  do not cover**. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G23] A10.5 stage 1 (signed 2026-10-18) registers this reading as A9's meaning:
   914	  no pin at the height gives `UNVERIFIABLE / HEADERS_NOT_COVERED`, a header differing from its pin gives `INVALID /
   915	  HEADER_CONFLICTS_PIN`. [OWNER 2026-10-19, Levadura contested 2] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` the verdict for a header that … conflicts with a
   916	  pin", 2026-10-13.)*
   917	- **Coverage.** Required: headers h* … h*+5 of the selected attestation of each of the three proofs (receipt,
   918	  snapshot, git), all in the shared `headers` pool. Every supplied header, used or not, is checked against the pins.
   919	  A `headers` entry that fails row 2.4 is not a supplied header: it contributes `CONTAINER_SCHEMA` only, and rows
   920	  10.x, 11.x, 13.4 and 14.6 use the remaining entries [OWNER 2026-10-14, diff-1 F-3].
   921	  For each pair of supplied headers at consecutive heights, a prev-hash mismatch gives `INVALID /
   922	  HEADER_CHAIN_BROKEN`; a missing intermediate height is not a broken link. No PoW, nBits or retarget check: a hash
   923	  equal to a pin implies all of it. At an unselected height, a pinned header with missing successors is ignored,
   924	  while an unpinned header blocks the bundle (`HEADERS_NOT_COVERED`). [OWNER 2026-10-14, G07, G05] *(Was: "`OPEN
   925	  (step 8):` which heights the pins must cover for a given proof", 2026-10-13.)*
   926	
   927	### 7.3 The DNSSEC snapshot rule
   928	
   929	- The issuer anchors the DNSSEC snapshot with its own OTS proof. The envelope is ENC §6 under the tag
   930	  `tessera-v1/dnssec-snapshot`. [DD:94; ENC §6:536-542]
   931	- The verifier checks that the snapshot's anchored time lies inside every RRSIG window in the chain and within
   932	  [declared − ε, declared + δ]. The issuer re-captures before shipping if it does not. [DD:94] This is an owner
   933	  decision carried in, not routed. [A9:99-101]
   934	  - "declared" is the receipt's signed `declared_issue_time`, not the snapshot's capture time. Capture-to-anchor
   935	    latency is a separate observation, not this predicate. (`docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F6, accepted 2026-10-13.)
   936	  - "every RRSIG window in the chain" means the one witness signature selected to authenticate each required RRset,
   937	    not every signature offered: an extra expired or invalid signature beside a valid one neither fails the RRset
   938	    nor narrows the joint window. The instrument's selection order is (key tag, inception, expiration, signature
   939	    bytes), first that validates. (`docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F5, accepted 2026-10-13.) Exception: an RRSIG
   940	    with fewer Labels than its owner fails the body restriction wherever it appears (section 6.1).
   941	    [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F07]
   942	  - **Frozen witness rule.** The selected witness is the **first eligible validating** candidate in (key tag,
   943	    inception, expiration, signature bytes, canonical RRSIG RDATA, canonical key RDATA) order. For the root, reliance
   944	    eligibility precedes selection. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G35] *(Previously described only as the instrument's order.)*
   945	  - Conjunct 3 (`confirmed_at` of the snapshot ≤ declared + δ) is a new owner acceptance restriction, fail-closed. A
   946	    failure of any conjunct gives `INVALID / SNAPSHOT_TIME`. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G06]
   947	- The chain is evaluated at the snapshot's anchored time, not at T of the receipt. [A9:137]
   948	- Why: the joint RRSIG window measured 1.6 days, shorter than δ (M8). [DD:93]
   949	- **Snapshot body.** A fixed-order sequence of uncompressed RFC 1035 wire RRs in RFC 4034 §6.2 canonical form,
   950	  section 15.3. Framing violations are `INVALID / SNAPSHOT_FORMAT`; a syntactically complete body lacking a required
   951	  RRset or its witness RRSIG is `UNVERIFIABLE / DNS_EVIDENCE_MISSING` (root: `NO_TRUSTED_KSK_USABLE`). The conversion
   952	  from resolver messages happens before stamping and changes S, so each snapshot needs a fresh stamp; earlier
   953	  stamped JSON snapshots cannot be reused. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G19] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` the snapshot body
   954	  format", 2026-10-13.)*
   955	- **Snapshot time.** T_snap = T of the snapshot's own proof under the section 7.1 rule, at the same depth k = 6, and
   956	  `confirmed_at`(snapshot) likewise. DNSSEC is evaluated at T_snap only; no capture time, receipt T or wall clock is
   957	  ever used. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G06] *(Was: "`OPEN (step 8):` how the snapshot's anchored time is derived",
   958	  2026-10-13.)*
   959	- **RRSIG time arithmetic.** Inception and expiration are 32-bit values v, each resolved to the unique absolute
   960	  second t with t ≡ v (mod 2^32) and |t − T_snap| < 2^31 (RFC 4034 §3.1.5, RFC 1982). If v − T_snap ≡ 2^31 (mod 2^32)
   961	  the candidate fails with `RRSIG_TIME_AMBIGUOUS`; if resolved inception > expiration it fails with
   962	  `RRSIG_INTERVAL_REVERSED`; inception = expiration is allowed. These are candidate failures under the existential
   963	  rule, ranked with `RRSIG_NOT_VALID_AT_EVAL_TIME`. The witness check is inception ≤ T_snap ≤ expiration, never "now",
   964	  including in 2106. [OWNER 2026-10-14, G27]
   965	
   966	### 7.4 The UTC assessment [OWNER 2026-10-19, A10.1.1]
   967	
   968	A10.1.1 (AUTHOR ADOPTED 2026-10-18, BROADEN; A10 rows 20–24) adds one reported assessment and one named assumption.
   969	Nothing in this subsection changes T, a verdict, a reason code, the check order or the stable field set.
   970	
   971	- **Assumption A-UTC** (`assumption_id` `"A-UTC-7200-6"`). At least one of the six blocks h* … h*+5 has a header
   972	  time no more than 7200 s before it was actually mined: `nTime(j) ≥ r(j) − 7200 s` for some j in [h*, h*+5], where
   973	  r(j) is the real (UTC) time block j was first produced. It is an assumption of the UTC assessment only, not a
   974	  Bitcoin consensus rule; **the verifier does not check it** and no bundle carries evidence of it. It is sufficient,
   975	  not minimal. [A10:280-285]
   976	- **Constants.** Δ = 7200 s and m = 6 are frozen catalog constants (section 1), not trust-configuration data, and
   977	  are not shown to be optimal. m = 6 is the same six-header segment h* … h*+5 that section 7.1 already requires and
   978	  pins (k = 6), so U needs no new input. [A10:284-285; A10 row 22; A10:343-344] *Instrument note: the TEST catalog
   979	  wire object (section 3.5; `prototype/differential/ADAPTER.md` T2) does not yet carry Δ and m; both instruments
   980	  compile them in. Adding them to the frozen catalog object is owner work before the freeze.*
   981	- **h\*** is the receipt proof's selected inclusion height (section 7.1, A10.5); it counts as confirmation one, so
   982	  h*+5 is confirmation six. [A10:277-278]
   983	- **Definition.** `U_seconds = 7200 + max(uint64(nTime(h*)), uint64(nTime(h*+1)), …, uint64(nTime(h*+5)))`, the
   984	  maximum over the receipt proof's selected h* and its six qualified headers (section 7.1 items (a)–(d)). Header
   985	  times need not be monotone: the maximum is taken over all six, not nTime(h*+5) and not T. Each nTime is the
   986	  header's unsigned 32-bit little-endian field (section 7.1 "Time arithmetic"), widened to 64 bits **before** the
   987	  addition; the sum is never reduced modulo 2^32. [A10:287-293; A10:307-311]
   988	- **Encoding.** `utc_upper_bound` is the RFC 3339 UTC string of U_seconds in the form used for T
   989	  (`YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`, days-from-civil, no leap seconds). For max nTime = 4294967295, U = 4294974495 =
   990	  `2106-02-07T08:28:15Z`, not a 1970 time. [A10:309-311]
   991	- **Null rule.** `utc_upper_bound` is `null` exactly when T is `null` (section 3.4), including every D1–D4
   992	  dispatch-terminal outcome; otherwise it is computed from the same selected h* that gives T. Every receipt-verdict report,
   993	  including a D1–D4 terminal report, contains `utc_assessment`, with `assumption_id`. For D1–D4 its
   994	  `utc_upper_bound` is null. Startup refusals produce no receipt-verdict report. [A10:300-302; OWNER 2026-10-19,
   995	  PR-03] *(Was: "The object `utc_assessment`, with `assumption_id`, is present on every report reached after
   996	  dispatch.")*
   997	- **No verdict effect.** U never enters a verdict, reason code, stable field, the shipping predicate or S; a value
   998	  is reported whatever the verdict, and with `INVALID` or `UNVERIFIABLE` it does not establish a valid service
   999	  receipt or valid service signatures. [A10:302-305; A10:347-348; A10 row 21]
  1000	- **Clock.** Verification does not consult the current clock; the arithmetic covers preserved headers. Timestamp
  1001	  representation for Bitcoin blocks produced after the 2106 rollover of the 32-bit field is a Bitcoin protocol
  1002	  matter outside V1. Section 7.3's serial-time unwrapping applies to DNSSEC RRSIG fields only, never to nTime.
  1003	  [A10:307-313; A10 row 24]
  1004	- **Claim, as registered** (A10:289-293): under A-UTC, the bytes and both signatures existed before U, in UTC. A2.1's
  1005	  refusal to bound the observation lag B − C is unchanged; guarantee 2 keeps its chain-time meaning (A10 row 23).
  1006	  A-UTC is false exactly when every one of the six blocks has a header time more than 7200 s before its production;
  1007	  then the conditional UTC guarantee is unavailable, though the computed value need not itself be false (A10 row
  1008	  24).
