  1680	### 15.5 Trust configuration [OWNER 2026-10-14, G08, G09, G22]
  1681	
  1682	One RFC 8785 JSON object, no numbers, every key required; authenticated only by the release allowlist (section 5).
  1683	
  1684	**Closure and grammar** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F14]. The schema is closed recursively: every object at every level
  1685	(top level, KSK entries, repository entries, `sources` items) has exactly the listed keys. Parsing is strict UTF-8
  1686	and JCS with no JSON numbers; binary values are canonical base64 (section 15.1); times use the `utc` grammar of
  1687	section 10; decimal strings are canonical unsigned decimals (`0|[1-9][0-9]*`) with per-field ranges: `version` 1 to
  1688	4294967295; `key_tag` 0 to 65535; `algorithm` 0 to 255; `reliance_lifetime_s` 1 to 2592000 (**owner choice**,
  1689	fail-closed: at most 30 days; the observed value is 1814400); heights as in section 15.1. `root_ksks` is sorted by
  1690	(`signing_start`, numeric `key_tag`, `dnskey_rdata` bytes); `repository_keys` by (`signing_start`, `key` bytes).
  1691	`root_ksks`, `repository_keys` and every `sources` array are nonempty, and every key in them valid (section 8). An
  1692	entry with `signing_end` null must have `signing_start` ≤ `as_of`. A schema failure is a startup refusal
  1693	(`TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`, no verdict), never a bundle `INVALID`. Cross-release invariants stay in the
  1694	release checker; the offline verifier never fetches a previous config. *(Dated correction 2026-10-14: "every key
  1695	required" did not say closed at every level, and the sort and decimal rules were less concrete than section 15.1.)*
