  1847	### 15.8 Spec and vectors object assembly [OWNER 2026-10-14, G13]
  1848	
  1849	Each object is one or more parts. A part is the ASCII line `tessera-v1-part <path> <length>` LF, then exactly
  1850	`<length>` bytes (decimal, no leading zeros), then LF. `<path>` is repository-relative UTF-8 without spaces. Parts are
  1851	in bytewise-sorted path order; file bytes verbatim as committed. The spec object holds the frozen
  1852	`docs/V1-PROFILE.md` and `formal/p8/ENCODING-V1.md` (plus the branch-registration supplement under the hybrid). The
  1853	vectors object holds every file under `formal/p8/vectors/` and the non-P8 conformance vectors (RES §L). The vectors
  1854	may embed the spec hash; the spec never embeds the vectors' hash.
  1855	
  1856	**Canonical recipe** [OWNER 2026-10-14, r2-F13]. Supersedes the header and inventory sentences above where they
  1857	differ.
  1858	- **Header grammar.** `tessera-v1-part` SP `<path>` SP `<length>` LF, with fixed single literal spaces. `<path>` is an
  1859	  ASCII repository-relative path matching `[A-Za-z0-9._/-]+`, with no empty, `.` or `..` component, no leading
  1860	  slash, and unique within the object; `<length>` matches `0|[1-9][0-9]*`.
  1861	- **Bytes.** Only tracked regular-file **blob bytes** from a named freeze commit: no newline conversion, no symlink
  1862	  following, no generated caches, no working-tree overlay.
  1863	- **Inventory.** The freeze record publishes the exact sorted path inventory of each object, including the non-P8
  1864	  conformance vector files (RES §L names cases, not paths), with an assembler and an independent byte-agreement
  1865	  check.
  1866	- **A9 is a part (owner choice).** The spec object also carries the governing A9 bytes
  1867	  (`docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-9.md` at its signing commit) as a part, so the object is the complete archival
  1868	  spec; this was chosen over distributing A9 separately with a bound digest.
  1869	- **A10 and the current proof are parts** [OWNER 2026-10-19, PR-08]. The spec object also carries, as parts, the
  1870	  signed A10 bytes (`docs/phase-0-prereg-amendment-10.md`, signed by the author's commit `9ade764`; this includes
  1871	  A10.4.2's required custody-procedure contents) and the current repaired `formal/p8/PROOF.md`, each taken at the
  1872	  freeze commit, whose A9 and A10 bytes must equal those at their signing commits. The "complete archival spec"
  1873	  sentence above holds only with these parts. HYBRID-SUPPLEMENT §9's table records the older baseline proof hash
  1874	  (`06910a5f…`); the current proof's SHA-256 is recorded in the freeze record, beside that baseline, outside every
  1875	  included part.
  1876	- **Hashes live outside.** Actual spec, vectors and catalog hashes are recorded in a separate release/freeze record
  1877	  **outside** the frozen spec parts; recording them inside an included file would be self-reference.
  1878	- The part format is a new packaging choice, not an inherited P8 construction or a proven archival format. Once
  1879	  exact catalog hashes exist, runtime hash checks are unique; runtime never parses these objects as policy.
  1880	  *(Dated correction 2026-10-14: "repository-relative UTF-8 without spaces" admitted LF, CR, tab, absolute and
  1881	  dot paths and duplicates; "every file under formal/p8/vectors/" did not select tracked regular files; "the
  1882	  non-P8 conformance vectors (RES §L)" named cases, not files.)*
  1883	
  1884	### 15.9 Release format [OWNER 2026-10-19, §14.2 item 2]
  1885	
  1886	Source: `docs/V1-FREEZE-OPEN.md` §3 (proposal R1–R6) as adjusted by its "Owner decisions" (2026-10-19); A10.4.2
  1887	(rows 34 and 36); the custody draft `docs/V1-RELEASE-KEY-CUSTODY-DRAFT.md` §2–§3. **Frozen at the 30 Oct freeze:** the
  1888	envelope below (archive, release manifest, signature, TEST/production rules, bootstrap rule). **Not frozen:** the
  1889	verifier's code bytes and its inventory, which each release binds (owner decision 1), and the production key,
  1890	ceremony, bootstrap record and rehearsal, which come after the freeze and before the first public verifier release
  1891	(A10.4.2). None of this is a bundle-verifier rule.
  1892	
  1893	**R1. A release is one archive.**
  1894	- One file, `tessera-verifier-v1-<n>.parts` (production) or `tessera-verifier-v1-TEST-<n>.parts` (TEST); `<n>` is the
  1895	  canonical decimal release number.
  1896	- Its bytes are the section 15.8 part format (header grammar, sorted paths). Every part except `RELEASE.json` is
  1897	  copied from the named **source commit** (verbatim tracked regular-file blob bytes). `RELEASE.json` is generated
  1898	  deterministically from those bytes and the declared release metadata; `source_commit` identifies their source
  1899	  commit. The independent assembler reproduces the complete archive, including that generated manifest, and it is
  1900	  compared by hash [OWNER 2026-10-19, PR-02] *(Was: "verbatim tracked regular-file blob bytes at one named source
  1901	  commit. Nothing is generated during assembly; the archive is rebuilt from the source commit and compared by
  1902	  hash", which required `RELEASE.json`, a tracked file, to name the commit containing it)* (the section 15.8 assembler and independent byte-agreement check), which is the input the
  1903	  custody procedure's review-and-build step (A10.4.2 C01) needs.
  1904	- Required parts: `RELEASE.json` (R2); `trust.json`, the exact section 15.5 bytes; `catalog.json`, the exact frozen
  1905	  catalog bytes; `release_constants.py` (R2); the dependency lock (R6); the vendored SHA-1DC source with
  1906	  `SHA1DC-PIN.md`; `install.sh`; the relying-party text; the verifier code. No other part carries authority.
  1907	- The archive is unpacked only after R3 succeeds. Scripts run as `bash install.sh`, so no file mode is needed.
  1908	  *(The custody draft's `.tar` is replaced: the part format has one byte string per file set.)*
  1909	
  1910	**R2. The release manifest and how the trust configuration and catalog bind.**
  1911	- `RELEASE.json` is one closed JCS object with exactly these keys: `format` (`"tessera-v1-release/1"`), `namespace`
  1912	  (`"PRODUCTION"` or `"TEST"`), `release` (= `<n>`), `profile` (`"tessera-v1-strict"`), `source_commit` (40 lowercase
  1913	  hex), `trust_config_sha256`, `trust_config_version`, `catalog_sha256`, `freeze_record_sha256`, `parts` (an object
  1914	  mapping every other part's path to the 64-lowercase-hex SHA-256 of its bytes, exactly the archive's other paths),
  1915	  and `release_key_successor` (40 uppercase hex v4 fingerprint, or null). All values are strings except `parts` (an
  1916	  object of strings) and a null successor. `parts` is how a release binds its verifier code (owner decision 1).
  1917	- **Binding equalities**, checked before signing and again by `install.sh`; any failure means no installation:
  1918	  every `parts` entry equals the hash of that part and the path sets are equal; SHA-256(`trust.json`) =
  1919	  `trust_config_sha256` = `release_constants.TRUST_SHA256`; SHA-256(`catalog.json`) = `catalog_sha256` =
  1920	  `release_constants.CATALOG_SHA256`, and `release_constants.CATALOG_BYTES` equals `catalog.json`; the `version`
  1921	  inside `trust.json` = `trust_config_version`; `release_constants.NAMESPACE` = `namespace`. Under `PRODUCTION`,
  1922	  `catalog_sha256` equals the catalog hash in the freeze record whose SHA-256 is `freeze_record_sha256`.
  1923	- Run time is unchanged: a `--trust` file whose hash is not `TRUST_SHA256`, or that fails section 15.5, is
  1924	  `TRUST_CONFIG_UNAUTHENTICATED`, no verdict. Each new release's `trust.json` passes the release checker (section 5
  1925	  release invariants, EA-12) against the previous release's.
  1926	- **No machine-specific facts in the signed archive.** `release_constants.py` holds only `NAMESPACE`,
  1927	  `TRUST_SHA256`, `CATALOG_SHA256`, `CATALOG_BYTES` and `UNICODE_VERSION` (`"16.0.0"`), all independent of the build
  1928	  machine. Facts about one installation (the interpreter's `unicodedata` file hash, whether SHA-1DC built) are
  1929	  written by `install.sh` into a per-installation record `install.json`, outside the archive and unsigned; the
  1930	  verifier checks them at start (Python 3.14, Unicode 16.0.0, SHA-1DC present, else the existing startup refusals)
  1931	  and reports the SHA-256 of `install.json` as a diagnostic. `install.json` carries no authority. *(Closes the
  1932	  triage's departure: `build_release.py` wrote `UNICODE_MODULE_SHA256` and `SHA1DC_AVAILABLE` from the build
  1933	  machine into the release.)*
  1934	
  1935	**R3. The release signature.**
  1936	- A **detached, binary (unarmored) OpenPGP v4 signature of type 0x00** over the exact archive bytes, file
  1937	  `<archive>.sig`, ECDSA P-256 with SHA-256, satisfying rule O's grammar and policy (section 8). The signing session
  1938	  checks it against rule O before it leaves the session.
  1939	- The release key is **one v4 ECDSA P-256 primary key with no subkeys**, identified by its full v4 fingerprint. A
  1940	  signature by any other key, or by a subkey, authenticates nothing.
  1941	- The signature covers the whole archive, code included. The key never signs a hash written as text.
  1942	- **Acceptance.** Accept only if a trusted acquisition-side checker first validates exactly one unarmored
  1943	  signature against rule O's grammar and section 8 policy, confirms the authenticated primary-key identity (the
  1944	  currently authenticated production key under R5: the bootstrapped key or an authenticated successor), and the
  1945	  pinned `gpgv` verifies the exact archive bytes with a keyring holding **only** that key. Installation then
  1946	  requires R2's equalities. The checker must be available before executing the acquired archive. [OWNER
