   395	## A10.4 Authority capture: honest signed material protected; publication remains an issuer duty; key substitution — AUTHOR ADOPTED 2026-10-18: option (e) (recommended by the owner; option (e) also by Codex), NARROW-CLAIM and BROADEN (review A04)
   396	
   397	A9's adversary sentence includes "anyone who captures authority-channel credentials while signing capability stays
   398	uncompromised (A8.3's sense of capture)" (:41-42). It names no set of receipts and no object for "signing
   399	capability". A8.3 (:72-76) bounds the tested property: under capture, "a captor cannot reattribute honest frames,
   400	alter honest signer sets, or carry honest signatures onto other bytes or keys". That property concerns honest
   401	service material, not only receipts issued before disclosure; the capture fixtures disclose the credentials before
   402	the honest signing processes run. A8.5 (:123-124) puts "preventing a captured or merged authority from issuing new
   403	attestations" out of scope. A captor holding both required channel credentials can sign and anchor a key assertion
   404	that names its own keys, without publishing it; receipts under those keys can then pass V1. Capture of only one
   405	required channel does not generally suffice.
   406	
   407	**The author's standard** (2026-10-08, `docs/guarantee-tiers-DRAFT.md`, "Authority capture"): *"perhaps the right
   408	way to attack the problem is distribution and admission that governance changes are out of scope"*, and
   409	*"Authority capture must be visible in such instances. For an adjudicator to be convinced incorrectly requires the
   410	takeover be invisible."* The owner summarizes it as: prevent, no; rewrite the past, no; visible, yes. The summary
   411	is the owner's, not a quotation. These words are the motivation for A10.4. They are not evidence that the visibility
   412	property is established. No option below establishes it: option (e) prevents acceptance by a scoped set of relying
   413	parties; it does not ensure that anyone detects or publicly exposes a capture.
   414	
   415	**Recommended text.** Replace the adversary clause with:
   416	
   417	> anyone who captures authority-channel credentials (the DNS credential or the repository key, including both
   418	> together) while the original service keys stay secret and their signing capability stays uncompromised (A8.3's
   419	> sense of capture), as to frames honestly signed by those service keys, whether before or after credential
   420	> disclosure: such a captor cannot reattribute those frames to other keys or another service, alter their honest
   421	> signer sets, or carry their signatures onto other bytes or keys (A8.3);
   422	
   423	and add to "The boundaries", before its last sentence:
   424	
   425	> V1 does not prevent a captor of both required authority-channel credentials from issuing new receipts under a key
   426	> assertion that names the captor's own keys, even while the original service keys remain uncompromised. Public
   427	> publication of the key-assertion repository remains an issuer duty (A9.7, "Changed in effect"). If the
   428	> substituted assertion is publicly published, a third party can inspect the substituted keys. The verifier does
   429	> not check publication; V1 does not guarantee that a captor publishes the assertion, that capture is detected, or
   430	> that new receipts are publicly visible.
   431	
   432	Under option (e), row 26 replaces this boundary's first sentence with a scoped one (A10.4.1).
   433	
   434	Backdating stays with the temporal checks (C4's chain-order pre-commitment, C5) under their own assumptions; A8.3's
   435	runs do not test it (A8.3). Whole-author-sphere capture stays excluded (R-2.15). Guarantee 2's proviso
   436	"provided the authority channels are not also captured" (:443) is unchanged and consistent with this text.
