     1	# The service-key listing is cut from V1 (A10 row 33)
     2	
     3	> **OWNER DECISION, 2026-10-19** (Claude Opus 5.5, owner instance). GATE action that the registered text already
     4	> provides: A10 row 33 makes the listing the second item cut and states what applies when it is cut. No amendment.
     5	> The author adopted option (e) on 2026-10-18, including row 33. He can override this cut until the freeze assembly
     6	> (26 Oct, `docs/V1-EXIT-30OCT.md`); after that, putting the listing back means a new spec object.
     7	
     8	## What is cut
     9	
    10	A10.4.1 and the rows that take effect only under option (e) with it: 25–32 and 37–39. These are the `service_keys`
    11	trust-configuration member, its classifier and reason codes, the listing pre-commitment, the C0/C1 bootstrap, and
    12	the listing-evidence package that relying parties would have to keep outside the bundle.
    13	
    14	## What applies instead (row 33)
    15	
    16	A10.4 option (a): honest signed material stays protected under authority capture (rows 15–16; contract G10). **V1
    17	gives no protection against new receipts under a captor's own keys** when both authority channels are captured, and
    18	the relying-party text and the freeze record say so. Release-key custody (A10.4.2) is unchanged: the cut does not
    19	cure a failure of release-key independence, and the custody gate at the first public verifier release still holds.
    20	
    21	## Why
    22	
    23	1. **Schedule.** The 30 Oct exit audit (`docs/V1-EXIT-30OCT.md`) puts the listing on the critical path, with about
    24	   one working day of slack. Cutting it saves about 4 days (owner estimate). The first cut item, the PQ hybrid, is
    25	   already registered and would save almost nothing.
    26	2. **What it buys is narrow.** It protects only relying parties whose verifier release is authentic. After review
    27	   r4 its protection against a later release-key theft needs independently authenticated Bitcoin history. It moves
    28	   trust from two credentials (DNS and repository key) to a third (the release key), and all three have the same
    29	   custodian.
    30	3. **What it costs relying parties.** A listing-evidence package outside the receipt bundle that holders must keep
    31	   (contract review VC-02). This is the one mechanism added purely for a decades-later adjudicator
    32	   (`docs/decisions/2026-10-19-horizon-cost.md`), and it is the one the author asked about and got no clear answer to.
    33	
    34	## What is lost, honestly
    35	
    36	- Production receipts issued before any later listing can never have its protection. A listing added in a later
    37	  version protects only receipts after it.
    38	- The horizon analysis recommended keeping the listing at its signed scope, on that ground. The owner weighs the
    39	  schedule and the relying-party burden above it for V1.
    40	
    41	## Registered as a candidate, not an obligation
    42	
    43	A key listing with a chain-order pre-commitment, or a witnessed log of key assertions, is a candidate for a later
    44	version. Neither creates an obligation.

