   253	## A9.4 Revocation and historical security: not evaluated — AUTHOR DECISION B; OWNER DECISION for historical security. A weakening under A1 §A1.1.
   254	
   255	- **Weakened.** For V1 bundles, `VALID_STRICT` evaluates neither P6 condition: `key_authorized(declared_issue_time)`
   256	  against effective revocation, and no revocation effective at or before `max(anchor_time, declared_issue_time)`
   257	  (A4.3). Every report carries `revocation: NOT_EVALUATED` next to the verdict. *Why:* under the registered text
   258	  a verifier that cannot evaluate revocation returns `UNVERIFIABLE` for every receipt.
   259	- **Historical security.** Every report carries `historical_security: NOT_EVALUATED`. V1 does not evaluate whether
   260	  a signing algorithm or key was safe when it signed. The documentation names what a later examiner must supply:
   261	  an authenticated safe-before cutoff per algorithm and per key, compared against T with A2.1's chain-time
   262	  uncertainty (CODEX-REVIEW 8). A surviving PQ attestation slot prevents a P-256 break alone from supplying every
   263	  required attestation signature. An ML-DSA-65 cutoff and the classical slot remain; historical authority, per-key
   264	  custody and continuing commitment and history security remain assumptions. A PQ slot never removes the need for
   265	  a theft cutoff.
   266	- **Issuer duty.** The V1 issuer never re-authorizes a key (R-5.24). V1 defines no revocation publication.
   267	- **Contract for later verifiers.** Missing status capture does not by itself invalidate a V1 receipt. A later
   268	  verifier given a V1 bundle, under the same governing V1 specification, policy, trust configuration and other
   269	  declared inputs, returns the same verdict and values in the stable field set (A9.1), and never a weaker verdict
   270	  because of missing status capture. **Verdict stability** (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13, `docs/V1-V2-COMPATIBILITY.md`): a V1
   271	  bundle is always evaluated under its signed `common.spec`. Trust configurations are append-only (A9.5), so a
   272	  later configuration cannot turn a V1 verdict into `UNVERIFIABLE` by dropping a KSK, a header pin or a repository
   273	  key. Later algorithm policy enters the separate historical-security assessment only as an authenticated
   274	  per-algorithm cutoff compared with T, with A2.1's chain-time uncertainty; authenticated per-key theft cutoffs are
   275	  assessed separately there as well. It never overwrites the V1 verdict or its six stable fields, whether T is
   276	  before, equal to or after a cutoff. A receipt whose T precedes an algorithm's cutoff keeps its V1 verdict; that
   277	  comparison alone does not establish historical safety. Later revocation, standing, renewal and historical-security
   278	  results are reported separately, together with later adverse evidence. The V1 verdict continues to apply the
   279	  algorithm policy registered for its governing V1 specification, including its original malformed, unsupported and
   280	  prohibited-identifier cases (A9.7 row 29). This
   281	  binds later verifiers and profiles; it is not a property V1 itself demonstrates. **This is not
   282	  non-repudiation.** Evidence (C7): for an otherwise valid, fully performable bundle where missing status capture
   283	  is the sole difference, a companion returning `INVALID` or `UNVERIFIABLE` must go red.
   284	
   285	## A9.5 Strict-only, standalone; the receipt anchor — OWNER DECISIONS 2026-10-12 except where marked
   286	
   287	- **Dispatch.** Out-of-profile input gives `UNVERIFIABLE / OUT_OF_PROFILE` at dispatch; A4.2 precedence holds inside.
   288	- **H1a's suite.** Forge, tamper, backdate, break-chain, strip-one-signature, out-of-profile, the A9.1 mapping.
   289	  Degradation and wrapper instances move to their capabilities.
   290	- **Receipt anchor.** OTS stamps `SHA256(A_sig)`, tag `tessera-v1/receipt-anchor-sig`, which commits to both frames
   291	  and their exact detached signatures in manifest slot order (ENCODING-V1 §5, D-8, D-14). The verifier verifies the
   292	  committed signature bytes only. The frame-only layout is withdrawn, with no fallback.
   293	- **Issue order (D-15).** Fix manifest and metadata; build frames; sign; verify; **freeze** the signature bytes;
   294	  **stamp** `A_sig` (one stamp per receipt, R-3.42); obtain confirmation; ship. A signature made after T cannot fit
   295	  the anchor. A retry that changes any committed byte needs a fresh stamp. Signature identifiers stay independent
   296	  of signature bytes; the anchor digest may commit to them; nothing is normalized after stamping.
   297	- **Git stamp target (D-16).** The git channel's OTS stamp targets the **signed commit's object id**, never a tree,
   298	  blob or unsigned commit. The verifier recomputes the id, verifies `gpgsig`, follows commit→tree→blob, and checks
   299	  the stamped id. SHA-1 linkage remains a stated residual. The git evidence names its object format, and V1
   300	  accepts only `sha1`: the key-assertion repository stays in SHA-1 format at a host that supports it, whatever
   301	  git's default becomes (the author, 2026-10-13: Git 3 moves to SHA-256 in 2027; GitHub does not support it).
   302	  The pre-freeze bundle schema (V1-PROFILE §2.1, G-26) defines the git-evidence member `object_format` as the string
   303	  `"sha1"`. It is an evidence descriptor, not a new signed-frame or key-publication field. A missing descriptor gives
   304	  `UNVERIFIABLE / GIT_OBJECT_FORMAT_MISSING`; an unsupported value gives `UNVERIFIABLE / GIT_OBJECT_FORMAT`. The
   305	  verifier recomputes the SHA-1 object ids from the raw commit/tree/blob bytes, so this descriptor cannot override
   306	  their interpretation. For V1 the stamped message is exactly 40 lowercase ASCII hex digits naming the signed commit
   307	  id followed by one LF byte. The schema/check specification and conformance cases are fixed before the 30 Oct
   308	  profile freeze; no P8 frame or key-publication vector changes are required.
   309	- **Root-KSK rule.** The root DNSKEY set is valid at the snapshot's anchored time **if some trusted KSK whose reliance
   310	  period contains that time validates it**. Each KSK entry records signing-start, signing-end and a sourced reliance
   311	  lifetime L, with the reliance interval [signing-start, signing-end + L]. L is the registered acceptance-policy
   312	  value derived from the cited root DNSKEY RRSIG validity evidence; a largest observed lifetime is labelled observed,
   313	  not asserted to be a proven global maximum. A candidate must also satisfy cryptographic validation and the selected
   314	  RRSIG's inception/expiration checks at the snapshot's own verified anchored time. This extension permits authentic
   315	  pre-retirement evidence anchored later; it does not establish that signing occurred before retirement. A thief of
   316	  a retired KSK can manufacture eligible root evidence during the extension. Exposure is bounded by the reliance
   317	  endpoint in snapshot chain time, not by a finite UTC duration under A2.1. Historical authority-key safety and the
   318	  continuing anchor/history assumptions remain necessary and are not evaluated by V1. **AUTHOR ADOPTED, 2026-10-13**
   319	  (`docs/reviews/v1-profile/` F2; kind CORRECT: the withdrawn cutoff claimed a protection DNSSEC itself does not
   320	  supply, since resolvers accept a retired KSK's signatures until they expire). The author: *"I'd argue that the
   321	  'narrowing of protection' wasn't a real narrowing, it was us admitting the real level of protection given the
   322	  constraints of the project."* The rule is existential and order-independent: a candidate that fails is
   323	  informational, and the result is computed once, after every eligible key has been tried (F1). A trusted KSK that
   324	  is published but did not sign is informational, never a failure. The trust configuration records signing and
   325	  reliance periods separately from publication and revocation dates.
   326	- **Trust-configuration authority** (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-14, after `docs/reviews/spec-sufficiency/` F04). The
   327	  trust configuration is authenticated independently of the bundle and of both authority-channel credentials. Each
   328	  verifier release compiles in the hash of its configuration. Releases are authenticated by a key that neither the
   329	  DNS credential, the repository key nor the issuing AWS account can use; the dated custody record names that key
   330	  and its access boundary. Capture of an authority-channel credential grants no ability to sign a release,
   331	  authorize a configuration hash, or replace the compiled trust root. CI is not the authenticity check. Compromise
   332	  of the release key is distribution compromise, a stated boundary.
   333	- **Trust configuration is append-only** (OWNER DECISION 2026-10-13). Trust configuration entries are append-only: KSKs, header
   334	  pins and repository keys are never removed, and finalized period endpoints never change. Repository trust entries
   335	  name D, the key and an inclusive signing interval; the git assertion passes this trust check if some configured
   336	  entry for D verifies gpgsig and its interval contains the signed commit's own verified anchored time. Repository
   337	  entries have no KSK-style reliance extension. Unknown material cannot establish this required check. Append-only
   338	  extensions preserve previously usable trust witnesses; newly covered evidence can improve an earlier UNVERIFIABLE
   339	  result. Exact stable-field identity remains the contract for identical declared inputs. Closing an open period must
   340	  not retrospectively exclude evidence previously accepted under that entry; the pre-freeze configuration records the
   341	  endpoint/update convention explicitly. **Rollover control:** a snapshot taken while one trusted KSK is published but not signing must pass
   342	  (SPIKE-REPORT, KSK-2024 re-capture; without it every receipt from 2026-10-11 would be falsely rejected).
   343	- **Custody.** V1 offers no renewal. Long-horizon validity depends on the dependency manifest and on the holder
   344	  keeping the exact bytes (a mirror that alters a signature byte breaks its copy, CODEX-REVIEW 5).
